      {
        "name": "prune",
        "label": "Projection",
        "description": "Backend ?prune=. true (default) is the connectivity-connected subgraph: a pod is kept only if it sits on a pod-calls-pod / pod-calls-service / service-selects-pod edge, and the node or claim holding only dropped pods goes with it. false returns the inventory instead \u2014 every loaded pod plus unreferenced infrastructure, which is what scripts/verify.sh asserts against.",
        "type": "custom",
        "query": "Traffic graph : true, Full inventory : false",
        "multi": false,
        "includeAll": false,
        "current": {
          "selected": true,
          "text": "Traffic graph",
          "value": "true"
        },
        "options": [
          {
            "text": "Traffic graph",
            "value": "true",
            "selected": true
          },
          {
            "text": "Full inventory",
            "value": "false",
            "selected": false
          }
        ],
        "hide": 0,
        "skipUrlSync": false
      },
      {
        "name": "alert_pod_list",
        "label": "Alert pod list (exported)",
        "description": "Written by the KSG panel after each graph load \u2014 pod names that currently have at least one alert (any severity); $__empty means a loaded graph with zero alerting pods. custom + multi + allowCustomValue so it can carry the multi-value writes this produces \u2014 a plain textbox variable cannot hold multiple values. This demo does not seed alerts, so the variable stays $__empty unless the running cluster produces some.",
        "type": "custom",
        "query": "$__empty",
        "multi": true,
        "allowCustomValue": true,
        "includeAll": false,
        "current": {
          "selected": false,
          "text": "$__empty",
          "value": "$__empty"
        },
        "options": [
          {
            "text": "$__empty",
            "value": "$__empty",
            "selected": true
          }
        ],
        "hide": 0,
        "skipUrlSync": false
      },
      {
        "name": "alert_names",
        "label": "Alert names (exported)",
        "description": "Written by the KSG panel after each graph load \u2014 distinct alert names found across all node kinds (pod/node/pvc/service/controller), any severity; $__empty means zero alerts in the loaded graph. custom + multi + allowCustomValue for the same reason as alert_pod_list. This demo does not seed alerts, so the variable stays $__empty unless the running cluster produces some.",
        "type": "custom",
        "query": "$__empty",
        "multi": true,
        "allowCustomValue": true,
        "includeAll": false,
        "current": {
          "selected": false,
          "text": "$__empty",
          "value": "$__empty"
        },
        "options": [
          {
            "text": "$__empty",
            "value": "$__empty",
            "selected": true
          }
        ],
        "hide": 0,
        "skipUrlSync": false
      },
      {
        "name": "selected_pod",
        "label": "Selected pod",
        "description": "Written by the KSG panel on left-click of a pod (single name) or controller (all child pod names), regardless of status. custom + multi + allowCustomValue so it can hold pod-name value(s) outside any fixed option list \u2014 a plain textbox variable cannot carry the multi-value writes a controller click produces. Consume from a sibling log panel, e.g. ${selected_pod:lucene}. $__empty means nothing selected yet.",
        "type": "custom",
        "query": "$__empty",
        "multi": true,
        "allowCustomValue": true,
        "includeAll": false,
        "current": {
          "selected": false,
          "text": "$__empty",
